Let $E$ denote the given expression. Use $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ first at $x=\frac{\pi}{24}$ and then at $x=\frac{\pi}{12}$. Each application introduces a factor of $\frac{1}{2}$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=4\,\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)\\&=2\,\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)=\frac{1}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=2\,\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)\\&=1.
\end{aligned}
$$

